1. Course Outcomes (COs)

Upon successful completion of this assessment, students should be able to:

    CO1: Describe the fundamental concepts and principles of software design.
    CO2: Explain the importance of software design principles in developing maintainable software.
    CO3: Analyze basic software design concepts, including coupling, cohesion, abstraction, and encapsulation.
    CO4: Explain common software architectural styles and design patterns.
    CO5: Apply fundamental software design principles to simple software problems.

2. Assessment Questions
Section A: Knowledge (C1 — Remember)

Total Marks: 10

Q1. Answer the following questions briefly. (5 × 2 = 10)

a) Define software design. (2)

b) What is modularity in software design? (2)

c) Define coupling. (2)

d) Define cohesion. (2)

e) What is a software design pattern? (2)
Section B: Understanding (C2 — Understand)

Total Marks: 15

Q2. Answer the following questions. (2 × 5 = 10)

a) Differentiate between coupling and cohesion in software design. (5)

b) Explain abstraction and encapsulation with suitable examples. (5)

Q3. What is the Model-View-Controller (MVC) architectural pattern? Explain the responsibilities of the Model, View, and Controller. (5)
Section C: Application (C3 — Apply)

Total Marks: 15

Q4. Answer the following questions. (2 × 5 = 10)

a) A Student Management System has separate modules for student registration, attendance management, and result processing. Explain how modularity improves the maintainability of this system. (5)

b) A software class performs several unrelated tasks. Explain how you would redesign it to improve its cohesion. (5)

Q5. You are designing a simple Online Shopping System. Identify three essential software modules and briefly describe the responsibility of each module. (5)
